contentText:
The surprising new collaboration between Kid Rock and Eminem on the former&#8217;s equally surprising new faith-based album Halfway 2 Jesus has taken some, well, surprising turns during the days before its Oct. 17 release.

When the album was announced earlier this month, accompanied by the single &#8220;James 1:17 (Jesus Is King),&#8221; Eminem was listed on the track &#8220;7 Sins.&#8221; Now he&#8217;s part of &#8220;Heal My Faith,&#8221; while Lil Wayne became a replacement for &#8220;7 Sins&#8221; &#8212; following some last-minute maneuvering.

&#8220;It&#8217;s a lot of stress and a lot of anxiety, but it&#8217;s so good,&#8221; Rock (real name Bob Ritchie) told Billboard during a visit this week with other reporters to his Dixie Ridge farm spread in Nashville, in the hills overlooking downtown (and next door to a new golf course built by Justin Timberlake).

&#8220;It&#8217;s giving me the exact record I wanted,&#8221; he says of the last-minute change. &#8220;I am so frickin’ stoked, and I feel like the biggest weight I have ever felt (doing an album) has been lifted. I have never experienced good anxiety and stress before.&#8221;

The switch-up came with a bit of comic, manic machinations that Rock said reminded him of making Devil Without a Cause , his 1998 breakthrough. &#8220;There was a lot of last minute (stuff) on that record, too,&#8221; Rock recalled. &#8220;I didn&#8217;t scream &#8216;My name is Kid!&#8217; until we were mixing the song (&#8216;Bawitaba&#8217;), &#8217;til we were in the mix room.&#8221;

What went down is that, despite their widely differing &#8212; and publicly expressed &#8212; political postures, Rock first approached Eminem about a year ago with &#8220;Heal My Faith,&#8221; previously titled &#8220;Lord Above&#8221; and &#8220;Show Me Love.&#8221; It continues a recording relationship that includes Eminem appearing on &#8220;F-ck Off&#8221; from Devil Without a Cause ; Rock popping into the 2013 video for Eminem&#8217;s &#8220;Berzerk&#8221;; and both appearing on Trick Trick&#8217;s &#8220;From the D&#8221; remix.

&#8220;We&#8217;ve been friends for, like, 40 years,&#8221; said Rock, an avowed friend and outspoken supporter of U.S. President Donald Trump and many in his administration. &#8220;I really didn&#8217;t want to drag him into my political world&#8230;. We talk about (politics) a little bit, not a ton. There&#8217;s a lot of stuff we agree on, and we can have a laugh about what (we don&#8217;t). That&#8217;s why I asked him to write a verse for me.&#8221;

Subsequently, however, Eminem heard &#8220;7 Sins&#8221; and decided he would rather rap on that. &#8220;We agreed we didn&#8217;t need two songs together&#8230;people are going to think we&#8217;re dating,&#8221; Rock said with a laugh. So Eminem cut a verse for that and the song was placed in the finished queue &#8212; only to have him reach out to Rock earlier this month, after the Halfway 2 Jesus tracklist was revealed. &#8220;He&#8217;s like, &#8216;Hey man, when&#8217;s the last time you listened to that first song we were working on?'&#8221; Rock said. &#8220;I&#8217;m like, &#8216;It&#8217;s been a minute&#8230;I&#8217;ve been so focused on this record.&#8217; I know I always loved it&#8230;and he listens to it and goes, &#8216;I think we should switch songs.&#8217; Which is a blessing. I&#8217;m like, &#8216;Oh my gosh, this is unbelievable.&#8217;

&#8220;He sends me back a verse wrapping up our whole history from like, day one &#8212; living at the Oakwood Apartments together, how I came to his Slim Shady release (show) when he was nobody and told him to keep going and how much that meant to him back then. He nails the line, &#8216;He&#8217;s my brother, not my political enemy,&#8217; and it just sent these chills. I&#8217;m like, &#8216;Man, this is so important.&#8217; I heard the (finished) mix, and grandpa was crying.&#8221;

That arrived on Oct. 11 and set off a scramble to fill the void in &#8220;7 Sins,&#8221; though Rock resisted the temptation to rap something himself; &#8220;Once (Eminem has) done something, I&#8217;m like, even if it&#8217;s OK&#8230;it&#8217;s not even close to what this kid does.&#8221; He had also created &#8220;a great musical break&#8221; for &#8220;7 Sins&#8221; but nevertheless reached out to both Lil Wayne and Chuck D; the latter couldn&#8217;t do it, but Wayne &#8212; also a friend of long-standing &#8212; assured Rock that, &#8220;I&#8217;ll knock it out today&#8221; and did, in fact deliver in relatively short order.

Keeping The Faith

So all&#8217;s well that ends well, and frantic, for Halfway 2 Jesus, Rock&#8217;s 13th album and the follow-up to 2022&#8217;s angry, politically charged Bad Reputation . &#8220;I said back then, in a social post, &#8216;I&#8217;m going to do this record. I&#8217;m going hard&#8230; and the one after this I&#8217;m gonna try to lower the tone and get back to some things that matter,'&#8221; he said. &#8220;So I was even thinking of it back then.&#8221; Rock feels the idea of he and Eminem putting aside their political differences to collaborate on Halfway 2 Jesus is a microcosm of the new album&#8217;s intent.

&nbsp;&#8220;This (album) is about forgiveness and second chances,” Rock said, “trying to heal some of the division in our country. I&#8217;m just putting a positive message out there in the world&#8230;showing a little more grace, trying to kill some of the division. I am not without sin, and I can&#8217;t say I ever will be. But I&#8217;m making a conscious effort to spread this better message &#8212; and I&#8217;m also still Kid motherf&#8211;king Rock. At the end of the day, I’m both of those guys. I&#8217;m not fundamentally changed, you know? I&#8217;m still Kid Rock; I&#8217;m gonna drink a beer, smoke a cigar&#8230; I&#8217;m just on the journey. I feel halfway to Jesus; I hope to be all the way there, but I&#8217;m not sure. I don&#8217;t know where it all leads. It&#8217;s almost as new as me as it is to everyone else.&#8221;

Rock &#8212; who was raised and still considers himself Catholic but is drawn more to the non-denominational Christian churches he occasionally visits in Nashville &#8212; noted that despite his prevailing American Badass posture he&#8217;s always incorporated faith into his songs, most notably &#8220;Only God Knows Why,&#8221; &#8220;Amen,&#8221; &#8220;Lonely Road of Faith&#8221; and &#8220;When U Love Someone.&#8221; (He also declared himself Rock n Roll Jesus on his Billboard 200-topping 2007 album, but that&#8217;s another matter.)

&#8220;I had a lot of friends pushing me to make a gospel record, and I always thought it&#8217;d be cool. I always loved when Johnny Cash did it, and Elvis (Presley) did it,&#8221; he said. He was already working on a more straightforward album &#8212; &#8220;I&#8217;ve got some of those great, crazy-ass Kid Rock songs sitting there and some crazy rap sh-t&#8221; &#8212; but was inspired to focused on Halfway 2 Jesus by a confluence of events: the widow of the late Charlie Kirk forgiving his killer; longtime Twisted Brown Trucker band keyboardist Jimmie &#8220;Bones&#8221; Trombly retiring due to ALS; and Rock&#8217;s father&#8217;s death in February.

&#8220;The time feels right,&#8221; Rock said. &#8220;I think people have had enough of the polarization, being so divided. And I think I can do my little part to help.&#8221;

Not Backing Down

He remains divisive, however &#8212; and fully acknowledges that. Since he announced the album, Rock appeared on Fox News with Lara Trump, slamming Michigan Democratic senatorial candidate Abdul El-Sayed as &#8220;a wolf in sheep&#8217;s clothing&#8221; and declaring that &#8220;I don&#8217;t want my grandkids to one day have to fight some civil war over religious ideology.&#8221; A few days later Rock introduced Trump at an Oct. 10 rally in Clarksville, Tenn., where he spoke about &#8220;Islamic extremists whose goal is to impose Sharia law on thi…
